    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p class="lead">How do insurance adjusters get paid? It depends on who signs the check. Staff adjusters receive a regular salary from the insurance company, independent adjusters invoice per claim or per day through an IA firm, and public adjusters take a percentage of the policyholder's settlement.</p>

<div class="callout"><strong>Quick answer:</strong> Staff adjusters are W-2 employees paid a salary on a normal payroll schedule. Independent adjusters are usually 1099 contractors paid a fee per closed claim (based on the gross loss) or a daily rate, often two to six weeks after the file is approved. Public adjusters earn a contingency fee, typically 10% to 20% of the settlement.</div>

<p>Each model affects not just how much you earn, but when the money arrives, what you have to pay for yourself, and how you handle taxes. Here is how each one actually works in practice.</p>

<h2 id="w2-salary">How do insurance adjusters get paid at an insurance company?</h2>
<p>Staff (company) adjusters are W-2 employees who work directly for an insurer or a third-party administrator. They get paid the way most office employees do:</p>
<ul>
<li>A fixed annual salary, paid weekly, biweekly or semi-monthly</li>
<li>Taxes withheld automatically, with the employer paying its half of Social Security and Medicare</li>
<li>Benefits such as health insurance, a 401(k) match and paid time off</li>
<li>For field staff, a company car, fuel card, laptop and phone, so there are few out-of-pocket costs</li>
</ul>
<p>The paycheck is the same whether you close 10 claims that week or 40. Some carriers add annual merit raises, bonuses tied to company results, or extra pay during catastrophe duty. Trainees and some desk roles are classified as non-exempt, which means overtime at time-and-a-half beyond 40 hours.</p>
<p>A common question is whether staff adjusters earn more for paying less. At reputable carriers, they do not. Reviews usually center on cycle time, file quality, customer satisfaction and accuracy, and unfair claim settlement practices laws in every state prohibit misrepresenting coverage or refusing to pay valid claims. Metrics and incentives do exist at some companies, and any policyholder who suspects bad faith can contact their state insurance department.</p>

<h2 id="1099-fee-bill">Independent adjusters: the 1099 fee bill</h2>
<p>Independent adjusters (IAs) are hired by IA firms, which contract with insurance carriers to handle overflow and catastrophe claims. The payment chain looks like this:</p>
<ol>
<li>The carrier assigns a claim to the IA firm.</li>
<li>The firm assigns it to you. You inspect, write the estimate and submit the report.</li>
<li>The firm's QA reviewer checks the file and sends it to the carrier.</li>
<li>The carrier approves the file and pays the firm based on its fee schedule.</li>
<li>The firm pays you your split, commonly 65% to 75% of the fee.</li>
</ol>
<p>The fee is tied to gross loss. Small claims under $5,000 typically bill around $350 to $600, claims between $25,000 and $50,000 around $1,000 to $2,000, and large losses $3,000 or more. Because you are a contractor, nothing is withheld from those payments. See our <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/insurance-adjuster-pay-rate/">insurance adjuster pay rate</a> guide for a full sample fee schedule.</p>

<h2 id="daily-rate">Daily rate assignments</h2>
<p>Some IA work pays a flat amount per day instead of per claim. This is common for desk CAT assignments, commercial claims and staffing contracts where the adjuster sits in for carrier staff. Typical rates run $400 to $700 per day for desk and daily CAT work, and $500 to $800 or more for experienced field or commercial adjusters.</p>
<p>Daily-rate work is often paid on a weekly or biweekly schedule, which makes cash flow far easier than waiting on closed files. Some of these roles are W-2 temporary positions through a staffing firm rather than 1099 contracts, so confirm the tax setup before you start.</p>

<h2 id="contingency-fee">Public adjusters: contingency fees</h2>
<p>Public adjusters work for the policyholder. They sign a contract with the homeowner or business, prepare and negotiate the claim, and are paid a percentage of the final settlement, typically 10% to 20%. Many states cap the percentage. Texas limits public adjuster fees to 10% of the settlement, and Florida caps them at 20% generally and 10% on claims tied to a declared state of emergency in the year after the declaration.</p>
<p>The fee comes out of the insurance payment, often because the check is issued jointly to the policyholder and the public adjuster. If there is no recovery, there is usually no fee. Our <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/public-adjuster-salary/">public adjuster salary</a> guide shows the math, and the <a href="/public-adjuster-fee-calculator/">public adjuster fee calculator</a> lets you run your own numbers.</p>

<h2 id="texas-adjuster-pay">How Texas adjusters get paid</h2>
<p>Texas is among the largest adjuster job markets in the US, and all four pay models are common there. Many carriers and IA firms run large hubs in Dallas-Fort Worth, Houston, Austin and San Antonio, so staff W-2 jobs are plentiful. We estimate the Texas statewide staff median at about $76,800, in line with the national BLS median, with mid-career metro estimates of roughly:</p>
<ul>
<li>Austin ~$81,400, Dallas-Fort Worth ~$80,600 and Houston ~$79,900</li>
<li>San Antonio ~$74,500, Beaumont-Port Arthur ~$73,000 and Corpus Christi ~$71,400</li>
<li>Lubbock ~$69,100, El Paso ~$67,600 and McAllen ~$66,000</li>
</ul>
<p>For Texas independents, the storm calendar sets the pay calendar. Hail, wind and tornado claims peak from March through June across DFW, Central Texas and the Panhandle, so fee bills from spring deployments often land in early summer. Gulf hurricanes from June through November drive work in Houston, Galveston, Corpus Christi and Beaumont, and winter freezes can bring burst-pipe claims, as the February 2021 storm did.</p>
<p>Texas public adjusters need a public adjuster license from the Texas Department of Insurance (TDI), and their contingency fee is capped at 10% of the settlement. More detail is in our <a href="/insurance-adjuster-advice/insurance-adjuster-salary-texas/">insurance adjuster salary in Texas</a> guide.</p>

<h2 id="payment-comparison">How do insurance adjusters get paid? Four models compared</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="data-table">
<thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Who pays you</th><th>Basis</th><th>Typical timing</th><th>Taxes withheld?</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>W-2 salary</td><td>Insurer or TPA</td><td>Fixed annual salary</td><td>Weekly or biweekly payroll</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>1099 fee bill</td><td>IA firm</td><td>Fee per claim by gross loss, minus firm split</td><td>2-6+ weeks after file approval</td><td>No</td></tr>
<tr><td>Daily rate</td><td>IA or staffing firm</td><td>Flat amount per day worked</td><td>Usually weekly or biweekly</td><td>Depends on W-2 or 1099</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contingency fee</td><td>Policyholder, from settlement</td><td>Percentage of recovery</td><td>When the insurer pays the claim</td><td>No (if self-employed)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>

<h2 id="when-paychecks-arrive">When paychecks arrive: the cash-flow lag</h2>
<p>Payment timing catches many new independents off guard. Because many IA firms do not pay until the carrier approves the closed file, there is commonly a lag of two to six weeks, sometimes longer, between finishing the work and seeing the money. Files sent back for corrections restart part of that clock.</p>
<p>On a Houston hurricane deployment, that means you may work four or five weeks and fund your own hotel, food and fuel before the first large payment lands. Practical ways to manage it:</p>
<ul>
<li>Keep two to three months of living and travel expenses in reserve before your first deployment.</li>
<li>Submit clean, complete files to avoid QA rejections that delay payment.</li>
<li>Ask each firm about its pay cycle, whether it pays on submission, on approval or on a fixed monthly date.</li>
<li>Track every file with its gross loss, fee and submission date so you can reconcile what you are owed.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout callout-tip"><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Some firms advance a portion of fees or pay per diem on large deployments. Ask before you accept the assignment; it is much harder to negotiate once you are already on the road.</div>

<h2 id="expenses-and-taxes">Expenses and taxes for 1099 adjusters</h2>
<p>A 1099 fee looks generous until you subtract what an employer would normally cover. Independent adjusters typically pay for:</p>
<ul>
<li>Travel, lodging and meals during deployments</li>
<li>A vehicle, mileage and insurance</li>
<li>A ladder, harness, moisture meter, measuring tools and a camera</li>
<li>An Xactimate subscription and other estimating or reporting software</li>
<li>License renewals and continuing education in each state</li>
<li>Health insurance and retirement savings</li>
</ul>
<p>On top of income tax, you owe self-employment tax covering both halves of Social Security and Medicare, and you are expected to make quarterly estimated payments. Many adjusters set aside 25% to 35% of every payment. Legitimate business expenses are generally deductible, so keep receipts and mileage logs, and consider working with a tax professional who understands per diem and travel rules.</p>

<h2 id="which-model-fits">Which pay model fits you?</h2>
<p>If you need predictable income and benefits, start as a staff adjuster. If you can handle uneven cash flow and travel, independent and CAT work has a higher ceiling. If you are comfortable selling your services to property owners, public adjusting rewards that. Many adjusters move between models over a career. To compare them with real numbers, try the <a href="/salary-calculator/">adjuster salary calculator</a>, or read our main <a href="/">insurance adjuster salary</a> guide.</p>
<p>Every path starts with a license. Many adjusters choose the Texas all-lines license from TDI, the most popular designated home state license nationally because of its broad reciprocity, and TDI-approved certification courses can satisfy the exam requirement. You can take one at <a href="https://www.adjustingonline.com/courses/" rel="sponsored noopener" target="_blank">AdjustingOnline</a>. Use <strong>joe20</strong> for 20% off.</p>
